us  or graph honor for metric gathering and  visualization in your own clusters and  we also keep an aggregated view of this  so that we can see fleet wide  differences between cloud locations and  different kubernetes versions and  finally we have some probers running  outside of Google cloud platform that  are constantly creating and deleting  clusters so this helps to give us a  critical black box view into what a user  would see it's in order to gather this  data in every cloud location we have a  job running outside of GCP called the  monitoring server and this collects  metrics metadata and core component logs  from each master so our definition of  mass availability is based on cubes ETL  component statuses returning healthy for  every component and if availability is  down for a short period of time the  monitoring server will automatically  recreate the unavailable unhealthy  master VM and it can also resize the  master VM in order to handle higher  workloads and if these actions don't  work and the master continues to remain  under bay  and alert is sent to your friendly on  caller this can also happen if some  other metrics get into a bad state  according to some predefined conditions  such as at CD backups being too old or  master VM discs being too full and  depending on the severity of the  condition the alert can be either a  ticket or a page that requires an  immediate response and it might be  version specific location specific or  even cluster specific and we can also  get tickets from our frontline support  team if they're seeing urgent customer  issues and I think we're pretty lucky in  gke  that so many Googlers are contributors  to the open source project so when we do  see issues or bugs with an underlying  version of kubernetes we can pretty  easily get those fixes and so a common  workflow might be an on caller receives  an alert we would then check our  dashboards and metrics to see the vital  signs of the cluster look in the  playbook for 