on paths to  other parts of of cloud and also our  developers who have their own on-call  rotations for various subsystems within  gke and kubernetes that they have more  expertise on and after the incident it's  really important that we write a  post-mortem and even more importantly we  learn from those action from the action  items from the post-mortem and execute  on them to make sure that the incident  is not repeated and sometimes we even  share some of these details with our  customers so the timeline of the instant  the root cause and the action items that  we're taking so that's what we're doing  on our side to improve the resilience of  the system to wrap up as potential  regional cluster users what can what can  you do to help improve the resilience of  your cluster and also help us not to get  paged so in the open-source version of  this talk and that I gave it cube con  we'd talk about  ensuring that your @cd backups are done  frequently and that you also test the  restore from backup procedure frequently  as well but as I mentioned before in gke  this happens automatically for you and  similarly if you're running your own  master I'd be advising that you upgrade  frequently and keep on top of the latest  security fixes but again this is taking  care of you taking care for you in our  managed kubernetes products however as a  gk user you still have full control over  the upgrade cycle of your nodes and we  strongly recommend that you enable node  Auto updates so we can keep it in sync  with the master version as well as node  auto repair for fully self-healing  cluster we also recommend that your  provisioning your project to have the  quota and capacity to handle these  multiple multi zonal workloads and  something you can do to help the  Carini's scheduler out is to provide  resource limits for your pods and make  allow to schedule those a little bit  more intelligently as an application  developer it's important that you're  aware of those high availa